From 682fd8326e3cc32af086b33f1030e64a2539c84d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 11:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] DVDInputStreamNavigator: the soft telecine of the titles of a
 DVD image or folder is read before they play

The read ahead of the previous commit cannot be done for a disc: its
navigation (menus, stills, a change of title set) runs inside the reads of
the player. Here the titles are read by a navigator of their own.

CDiscSoftTelecineScan opens a second libdvdnav on the same image (a second
CDVDInputStreamFile) or folder when the player's navigator has opened the
disc, in a thread: it takes the titles of two minutes or more (the longest
twelve), plays each from its start with dvdnav_title_play(), takes the video
of the program stream (KODI::DISC::CVideoCollector: pack header with its
stuffing, packets of the first video stream, the PES header skipped) and
gives the picture headers to the detector of the previous commit, until it
knows or reaches 3 MiB of video, 4 s or 4000 events. Stills and waits are
skipped. A disc in a drive (a path of an optical device) is not read ahead:
a second reader would move the head against the player's.

CDVDInputStreamNavigator::GetSoftTelecine() answers for the title that plays
(waiting up to 1.5 s, once, if that title is being read now; a menu is "no").
The generation it gives changes with the title set and the title. The
demuxer asks at the first MPEG-2 video packet after a change
(ApplyDiscSoftTelecine()): for film the stream says 24000/1001, not
interlaced and softTelecine, and the player, which has not opened that
video yet, picks 23.976 Hz once, with the line in kodi.log; for what is not
film the stream is as it was, and what it said before is put back.

The detector is not told the end of a stretch: its last group of pictures
is cut where the reading stopped, and its last pictures in display order are
missing; counted, it looked like a break in the cadence (a closed-GOP film
was "not film" from the stretch, "film" from the file). Only complete groups
count, here and in the read ahead of files.

Tested with the real libdvdnav 7.0.0 and libdvdread 7.1.1 (built on the host
from LibreELEC's sources) on discs authored with dvdauthor and mkisofs from
MPEG-2 streams of the test corpus, as an ISO and as a VIDEO_TS folder: one
title of film; and a disc with a menu (VMGM that jumps to a title) and eight
titles: film with B pictures, hard telecine, video, film and then video,
video and then film, closed-GOP film, every flag set, 2:2. The ISO and the
folder give the same for all: film in the three that are film and in "film
then video"; not film in the rest; each in 28 to 34 pictures and about
1 MB of video. Six mutants of the collector and the scan fail the tests
(a unit test with sectors made by hand: stuffing, PES header length, audio
and a second video stream; the discs). The soft telecine timing of what
plays is the one of the previous commit (kodisim: 1 refresh per picture in 8
of 8 phases).

Not tested: a disc with audio (the corpus encoder of the host has no AC-3),
multi-angle titles, a disc in a drive, the player itself (nothing of Kodi runs
on the host): the demuxer and navigator parts compile and were read.
